==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[discuss]]; to [[converse]] about a matter or issue.
#: {{ux|en|Come home early, we have a lot to '''talk over'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1944|month=March and April|author=Chas. S. Lake|title=Some C.M.Es. I Have Known: VIII—Sir H. N. Gresley|journal=Railway Magazine|page=74|text=His reply was brief and to the point. "Come along to Doncaster," it read, "and we'll '''talk it over'''."}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[persuade]] (someone); to [[talk around]].
#: {{syn|en|talk around|win over}}
#: {{ux|en|I didn't like the idea at first, but they eventually '''talked''' me '''over'''.}}
#: {{ux|en| She is an expert at '''talk'''ing others '''over''' to her views/side.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To verbally interrupt (someone); to speak at the same time as someone else.
#: {{hypo|en|shout down#Verb}}
#: {{ux|en|She rudely '''talked over''' her opponent in the debate.}}

